  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how infused tumour cells travel through the body and reach a target tumour via the circulation. It contrasts two transport mechanisms, shown by different arrows: bulk blood flow (“convection”) moves cells over long distances through vessels, while local “diffusion” represents shorter-range spreading once cells are near or within tumour tissue.\n\nCells are introduced into the bloodstream at an infusion site, then carried to the heart and pumped through the lungs and systemic circulation. The looping vessels emphasize that the cells must pass through the cardiopulmonary system before reaching peripheral tissues. Near the target tumour site, a magnified view shows the transition from vessel-based transport to movement within the tumour microenvironment. In this inset, tumour cells appear to leave or approach the vasculature and then disperse among surrounding cells, where movement seems less directed and more limited than in the bloodstream.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that delivery of therapeutic or circulating tumour cells depends first on efficient vascular transport through the heart and lungs, and then on local penetration and spread in the tumour tissue itself, where distribution may become more constrained.",
